  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-f3800",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX F3800 Portable Power Station",
    "category": "3,840Wh LFP, 6,000W, TT-30R RV outlet",
    "badge": "Best Silent Hybrid",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Runs a 30-amp rig silently through quiet hours",
      "TT-30R outlet built in",
      "6,000W inverter handles AC start surges",
      "Expandable with add-on batteries",
      "No fumes, so it can sit near the rig"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Runs a rooftop AC for only a few hours on a charge",
      "About 132 pounds",
      "Costs more than any gasoline pick here"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best companion to a small generator, or a standalone for parks with strict generator rules.",
    "description": "The SOLIX F3800 is the way to camp with power during quiet hours and in generator-free loops. Anker rates its LFP battery at 3,840Wh and its inverter at 6,000 watts, and it carries a TT-30R outlet, so a 30-amp rig plugs straight in and runs everything, including a rooftop air conditioner, without a sound. The limit is capacity. An AC drawing around 1,400 watts drains it in a few hours, so on its own it is an overnight and quiet-hours solution, not an all-day one. It shines as half of a hybrid: run the battery through the night, then recharge it from solar panels or a small inverter generator during allowed hours. It also expands with add-on batteries for longer runs. At around 132 pounds it rides on its wheels and stays in the rig or truck. Because it makes no exhaust, it can sit beside the trailer rather than 20 feet away."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "RV Generators",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-rv-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size generator do I need to run a 13,500 BTU RV air conditioner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Without a soft starter, plan on about 3,000 running watts so the compressor can start reliably along with the converter and fridge. With a soft starter installed, many owners report a single 2,200-watt inverter starting a 13,500 BTU unit, though that leaves little margin. Heat and altitude both reduce generator output, so buy a size up if you camp in the desert or the mountains."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a 50-amp RV run on a 30-amp generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. A 50-to-30-amp dogbone adapter lets a 50-amp rig plug into a TT-30R generator outlet. You are then limited to about 3,600 watts, which typically means one air conditioner and careful use of the microwave and water heater on electric. Many 50-amp rigs have an energy management system that automatically sheds loads on a 30-amp supply, which makes the arrangement easy to live with."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a 30 amp RV plug adapter used for?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It usually adapts the rig's TT-30 plug to a standard 15 or 20-amp household outlet, or to a generator without a TT-30R. That works for lights, the converter and a fridge on electric, but limits you to roughly 1,800 to 2,400 watts, so the air conditioner is off the table. Adapters from 240-volt outlets need care; buy molded, labeled ones only."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is dual fuel worth it for an RV generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most RVers, yes. Running from the propane already on board eliminates gas cans, avoids stale fuel over the winter and simplifies refueling. The cost is lower output on propane, commonly around 10 percent, and faster tank use than many people expect. If your sizing is tight, check the propane running watts before relying on LP for air conditioning on hot days."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run my RV generator overnight at a campground?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not. Most campgrounds enforce quiet hours, commonly from around 10 p.m. to the morning, and many state and national parks restrict generators to set daytime windows. A battery power station or the rig's own lithium bank is the way to keep fans, the furnace blower and a CPAP running overnight, recharged by the generator during allowed hours."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are portable power stations for RVs good enough to replace a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For quiet hours, light loads and short trips with hookups between, they can be. A large unit with a TT-30R outlet runs the whole rig silently, but a rooftop AC drains even a 3,840Wh battery in a few hours. For multi-day boondocking with air conditioning, most owners still need a generator, ideally a small quiet one paired with the battery and solar."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How far should an RV generator be from the camper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "As far as practical. The CPSC recommends at least 20 feet from occupied spaces for portable generators, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and roof vents. At a tight campsite that can be hard, so use a longer heavy-gauge cord, buy a generator with automatic CO shutoff, and keep a working CO alarm inside the rig."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Power & Generators",
    tags: [
      "best rv generator",
      "rv generator",
      "30 amp rv plug adapter",
      "portable power stations for rvs",
      "rv appliances"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">RV Generators</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven generators for travel trailers, fifth wheels and motorhomes, chosen by the two things that actually decide the purchase: whether it starts your rooftop air conditioner, and whether it plugs into your rig without a mess of adapters.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#honda-eu3000is"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Compare the Specs
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#sizing-ac" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Sizing for the Rooftop AC</a>
          <a href="#plugs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. 30-Amp, 50-Amp and Adapters</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Seven Picks</a>
          <a href="#comparison" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Side-by-Side Comparison</a>
          <a href="#parallel" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Parallel Kits</a>
          <a href="#fuel" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Dual Fuel and RV Propane</a>
          <a href="#noise" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Quiet Hours and Campground Rules</a>
          <a href="#hybrids" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Power Station Hybrids</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Carbon Monoxide at the Campsite</a>
          <a href="#which-one" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Which One to Buy</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most RV generator advice starts with brand loyalty. It should start with one appliance. The rooftop air conditioner is the biggest, most demanding load on almost every rig, and if a generator cannot start it on a hot afternoon, nothing else on the spec sheet matters. Size for the AC first, match the plug second, and only then argue about Honda versus Champion.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="sizing-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Sizing for the Rooftop Air Conditioner
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical 13,500 BTU rooftop unit draws somewhere around 1,200 to 1,600 watts once the compressor is running, depending on the brand, the outside temperature and how hard it is working. A 15,000 BTU unit runs higher, commonly 1,500 to 1,900 watts. Those running numbers look easy for a 2,200-watt generator. The problem is the first second, when the compressor motor starts against pressure and briefly pulls several times its running current.
          </p>

          <p>
            That start surge is what kills small-generator plans. Depending on the unit, a 13,500 BTU compressor can momentarily demand 2,500 to 3,500 watts or more, and an older or tired compressor can be worse. An inverter generator responds by sagging, stalling or tripping its overload. This is why a single 2,200-watt inverter starting a rooftop AC without help is a coin toss, and why owners who say it works are often camping at sea level on a mild day.
          </p>

          <p>
            A soft starter changes the math. Devices such as the Micro-Air EasyStart and SoftStartRV are wired into the air conditioner and ramp the compressor up instead of slamming it on, which makers say cuts the start surge by more than half. With one installed, many owners report a 13,500 BTU unit starting reliably on a 2,200-watt inverter, and a 15,000 BTU unit starting comfortably on a 3,000-watt-class generator. Installation takes an hour or two for a handy owner, or a short shop visit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Without a soft starter, a sensible floor is about 3,000 running watts for a 13,500 BTU unit and 3,500 or more for a 15,000 BTU unit, which is why the 3,000 to 4,500-watt class dominates this list. Remember the AC never runs alone. The converter charging your house batteries, the fridge on electric mode, a coffee maker or a microwave all stack on top. A 30-amp rig tops out around 3,600 watts anyway, so that is the target most travel trailer owners are actually shopping for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Altitude and heat both cut generator output. Engines lose roughly 3 to 4 percent of their power per 1,000 feet of elevation, so a generator that just barely starts your AC in Florida may refuse in Colorado. If you camp high or in the desert in July, buy a size up or install the soft starter regardless of which generator you choose. It is the cheapest insurance in RV power.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plugs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 30-Amp, 50-Amp and the Adapters Between
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at the end of your RV's shore power cord before you look at generators. A 30-amp rig has a three-prong plug that fits a NEMA TT-30R outlet: 120 volts, 30 amps, about 3,600 watts maximum. A 50-amp rig has a four-prong plug that fits a NEMA 14-50R: two 120-volt legs at 50 amps each, about 12,000 watts in total. Nearly every RV-ready inverter generator has a TT-30R on the panel. Very few portables have a 14-50R, and those that do are big, loud, open-frame machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good news for 50-amp owners is that you do not need 12,000 watts to camp. A 50-amp-to-30-amp adapter, often called a dogbone, lets a 50-amp rig plug into a TT-30R generator. You then live within 3,600 watts: one air conditioner, not two, and no microwave while the AC is cycling. Many 50-amp fifth wheels have an energy management system that sheds loads automatically when it detects a 30-amp supply, which makes this arrangement easy to live with.
          </p>

          <p>
            The adapter question gets dangerous with 240-volt outlets. A generator's L14-30R twist-lock is a 120/240-volt outlet, and an adapter from it to a TT-30 plug must take only one 120-volt leg, which means your RV sees only half of the generator's output. Mis-wired or improvised adapters that send 240 volts into a TT-30 rig can destroy the converter, the fridge board and anything else plugged in. Buy a molded, labeled adapter, never a homemade one, and never confuse a TT-30R with a 240-volt NEMA 10-30 dryer outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Searches for a 30 amp RV plug adapter usually come from the opposite problem: plugging the rig into a household 15 or 20-amp outlet or a small generator with only standard receptacles. Those puck-style adapters work, but they limit you to roughly 1,800 to 2,400 watts, which will run lights, the converter and a fridge on electric, not the air conditioner. One more detail: many inverter generators have a floating neutral, and some RV surge protectors read that as an open ground and refuse to pass power. A neutral-ground bonding plug in a spare outlet solves it.
          </p>

