          <p>
            Two patterns jump out. First, the 3,000 to 4,500-watt inverters all land at or just above the 3,600 watts a TT-30R can deliver, which is why they are the default choice for 30-amp trailers. Buying more generator than your plug can carry only buys you headroom for altitude and heat, which is useful, but beyond that it is weight and fuel you are hauling for nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the only way to feed a 50-amp rig at full capacity from a portable is an open-frame generator like the WGen9500DF, and that comes with noise most campgrounds will not tolerate. If you camp in parks with neighbors, a 50-amp rig on a dogbone and a quiet 4,500-watt inverter is the better combination. Save the big open-frame unit for boondocking far from anyone else, or for home backup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight is the hidden spec. The single-unit 3,000 to 4,500-watt inverters all sit around 100 to 130 pounds, which means wheels, a ramp or a hitch-mounted carrier. If you cannot lift one into a truck bed, the parallel pair of EU2200i units, at about 47 pounds each, is the practical answer, even though it costs more for the same output.
