          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need to run a 13,500 BTU RV air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Without a soft starter, plan on about 3,000 running watts so the compressor can start reliably along with the converter and fridge. With a soft starter installed, many owners report a single 2,200-watt inverter starting a 13,500 BTU unit, though that leaves little margin. Heat and altitude both reduce generator output, so buy a size up if you camp in the desert or the mountains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 50-amp RV run on a 30-amp generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A 50-to-30-amp dogbone adapter lets a 50-amp rig plug into a TT-30R generator outlet. You are then limited to about 3,600 watts, which typically means one air conditioner and careful use of the microwave and water heater on electric. Many 50-amp rigs have an energy management system that automatically sheds loads on a 30-amp supply, which makes the arrangement easy to live with.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a 30 amp RV plug adapter used for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It usually adapts the rig's TT-30 plug to a standard 15 or 20-amp household outlet, or to a generator without a TT-30R. That works for lights, the converter and a fridge on electric, but limits you to roughly 1,800 to 2,400 watts, so the air conditioner is off the table. Adapters from 240-volt outlets need care; buy molded, labeled ones only.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is dual fuel worth it for an RV generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most RVers, yes. Running from the propane already on board eliminates gas cans, avoids stale fuel over the winter and simplifies refueling. The cost is lower output on propane, commonly around 10 percent, and faster tank use than many people expect. If your sizing is tight, check the propane running watts before relying on LP for air conditioning on hot days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run my RV generator overnight at a campground?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most campgrounds enforce quiet hours, commonly from around 10 p.m. to the morning, and many state and national parks restrict generators to set daytime windows. A battery power station or the rig's own lithium bank is the way to keep fans, the furnace blower and a CPAP running overnight, recharged by the generator during allowed hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are portable power stations for RVs good enough to replace a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For quiet hours, light loads and short trips with hookups between, they can be. A large unit with a TT-30R outlet runs the whole rig silently, but a rooftop AC drains even a 3,840Wh battery in a few hours. For multi-day boondocking with air conditioning, most owners still need a generator, ideally a small quiet one paired with the battery and solar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far should an RV generator be from the camper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As far as practical. The CPSC recommends at least 20 feet from occupied spaces for portable generators, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and roof vents. At a tight campsite that can be hard, so use a longer heavy-gauge cord, buy a generator with automatic CO shutoff, and keep a working CO alarm inside the rig.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
