        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy an RV generator by working backward from the rooftop air conditioner and the plug on your shore cord. For most 30-amp trailers, that leads to a 3,000 to 4,500-watt inverter with a TT-30R outlet, ideally dual fuel, and a soft starter on the AC for margin.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then think about the hours you cannot run it. A battery power station or a lithium house bank covers quiet hours and nights, and a quiet generator refills it during the day. That combination keeps you cool, keeps the neighbors friendly and keeps carbon monoxide well away from where you sleep.
          </p>
